# Learn Koka { .heading-no-number }

A fast-paced introduction for experienced programmers. Each page is
self-contained with runnable examples. If you prefer a thorough,
progressive treatment, see [The Koka Book](../tutorial/index.html).

---

## What Makes Koka Different?

Koka is a functional language where every side effect is tracked in the
type system. Functions declare exactly what they can do -- print, throw,
mutate state -- and the compiler enforces it. Effects are resolved using
_effect handlers_, which let you define control abstractions like
exceptions, generators, or async/await as ordinary library code.

Koka compiles to C without a garbage collector, using _Perceus_ reference
counting and _reuse analysis_ to turn functional code into efficient
in-place updates.

